That's interesting, because I'm also english, and I know that the English
muffin is indeed a "flat round bread", and not in fact a tea cake. The
difference being that a tea cake is sweet and usually contains dried
fruit, while an English muffin is savoury (and I suppose I can see why
some might think it tastes a little of paper).
They *are* both served toasted however - I remember enjoying English
muffins as a child, not so many years ago.

Unfortunately, the term "muffin" in England has rapidly become subsumed by
the American definition, apparently to the point where even citizens of
England have no idea what the original meaning is.
(Not that I have a problem with American muffins, of course...)

